WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.819
[MUSIC]

00:00:00.819 --> 00:00:06.356
Oftentimes, leaders see themselves as the
role of the hero, or as the protagonist

00:00:06.356 --> 00:00:11.988
of the story, and sees people that follow
them as bit players in the leader's play.

00:00:11.988 --> 00:00:13.404
[MUSIC]

00:00:13.404 --> 00:00:16.859
The Executive Program in Leadership
is a program designed to

00:00:16.859 --> 00:00:21.880
give people a set of skills and knowledge
to increase their leadership ability.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.660
To go from being emergent leaders,
moving more into a world

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:29.930
where they are leading big teams, whole
units, or perhaps whole organizations.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:31.428
What we're trying to do in EPL,

00:00:31.428 --> 00:00:35.570
it's help people understand that
everyone's the hero in their own story.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:37.070
And the more that you understand that,

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:40.970
the more you can internalize that belief,
I think the better leader you can be.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:43.489
&gt;&gt; I wanted to get a broader
perspective on leadership.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.230
&gt;&gt; Throughout my life, I've been leading
by instinct, I felt it is about time

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:53.540
I add to my leadership skills in
a structured formal environment.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.400
&gt;&gt; I wanted to attend for
my own personal development,

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.430
the betterment of our company,
and for the Stanford brand.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:03.920
&gt;&gt; One thing that's unique about Stanford
is that focus on experiential learning.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.410
Creating activities allow you to
experience the concepts that we're talking

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.610
about that more deeply
embeds the ideas into you so

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:12.580
that you can embody them when
you get back to the job.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.840
&gt;&gt; One of the things that
the program has taught me is that by

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:22.110
better understanding my own leadership
style, I am going to be more aware

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:26.470
of how other people experience me and how
I can hep them to become better leaders.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:30.500
&gt;&gt; The program is very good how to set
concrete steps towards achieving the goals

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:31.380
that you set for yourself.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.930
&gt;&gt; The ability to lead change is
a big part of the EPL program.

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:38.570
How do you engineer change in
the organization, change in the culture,

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:39.990
what constitutes the culture?

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.810
Making decisions about what kind culture
is most useful for your organization.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:48.170
To be an effective leader, you need to
both be able to understand yourself in

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:51.080
a deeper way, understand how other
people experience themselves.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:55.690
And then all those things together will
allow you to create a story, to craft

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:59.760
a persona, it allows you to lead the team
of the organization more effectively.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.010
&gt;&gt; There's so much that we are all
taking away from this experience.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:08.510
&gt;&gt; For anybody who hasn't been here, you
need to be at Stanford for this program.

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:12.010
I'm going to go home a better person,
stronger, more vibrant and

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:12.990
dynamic than ever.

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:16.378
I'm going to go home to make sure I bring
a change that will help transform my

00:02:16.378 --> 00:02:17.167
organization.

00:02:17.167 --> 00:02:20.831
[MUSIC]

